<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasdai Crescas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1340–<a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Hasdai ben Abraham Crescas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasdai ben <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraham">Abraham</a> Crescas was a Spanish-Jewish philosopher and halakhist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> around 1340, who served as crown rabbi of Aragon and taught pupils including Joseph Albo. In 1378 he was imprisoned with his teacher Nissim of Gerona on a false accusation of host desecration, and his only son was killed in the massacre of 1391, which he described in a letter to the congregations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a>. His Or Adonai (The Light of the Lord) became a classical Jewish refutation of medieval Aristotelianism, and he deeply influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baruch-spinoza">Baruch Spinoza</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1410 or the year after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasdai-crescas.jpg" alt="Hasdai Crescas" width="330" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moshe ben Maimon · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guide_for_the_Perplexed_by_Maimonides.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
